<p><strong>Terminology:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Parent: a node with one or more children.</li>
<li>Child: a node that has a parent.</li>
<li>Subtree: a tree formed by a node and its descendants.</li>
<li>Path: a single, continuous sequence of nodes connected by edges.</li>
<li>Depth: the number of edges from the root to a given node.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Attributes of a tree:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Root: the topmost node of the tree and the only node without a parent.<=
/li>
<li>Intermediate node: a node with a parent node and at least one child.</l=
i>
<li>Leaf: a node with no children.</li>
<li>Edge: the connection between two nodes. Trees usually have directed edg=
es, meaning the edges only point from parent to child.</li>
<li>Height: the length of the longest path from the root to a leaf.<sup><a =
id=3D"user-content-fnref-1" data-footnote-ref=3D"true" aria-describedby=3D"=
footnote-label" href=3D"https://bytebytego.com/courses/coding-patterns/tree=
s/introduction-to-trees#user-content-fn-1">1</a></sup></li>
</ul>
